<article class="env-lemma" id="0979">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0979">Lemma <span data-tag="0979">61.5.7</span>.</a> Let $A$ be a ring. Let $V(I) \subset \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(A)$ be a closed subset which is a profinite topological space. Then there exists an ind-Zariski ring map $A \to B$ such that $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(B)$ is w-local, the set of closed points is $V(IB)$, and $A/I \cong B/IB$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $A \to A_ w$ and $Z \subset Y = \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(A_ w)$ as in Lemma <a href="/tag/0975" data-tag="0975">61.5.3</a>. Let $T \subset Z$ be the inverse image of $V(I)$. Then $T \to V(I)$ is a homeomorphism by Topology, Lemma <a href="/tag/08YE" data-tag="08YE">5.17.8</a>. Let $B = (A_ w)_ T^\sim $, see Lemma <a href="/tag/096V" data-tag="096V">61.5.1</a>. It is clear that $B$ is w-local with closed points $V(IB)$. The ring map $A/I \to B/IB$ is ind-Zariski and induces a homeomorphism on underlying topological spaces. Hence it is an isomorphism by Lemma <a href="/tag/096L" data-tag="096L">61.3.8</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>